aid their rent in which case  they should suffer the consequences  through the courts of not paying their  rent  but that's a you know individual  judgments have to be made on this  but we are on the we are on course at  the moment  for a major problem because there's  about the estimates are 350 000 people  are in rent arrears right now and  potentially could be  evicted when the moratorium completely  ends  well if baron estrada was on this panel  one of the architects of universal  credit  she would say that it but it's it's a  feature not a bug in universal credit  but the rent is not paid directly to a  landlord  her argument would be that you know in  the same way that owner occupiers  pay their mortgage out of their salary  you should be helping people to learn  how to budget by having them  pay their landlord for their rent rather  than the state doing so directly  what would you say to that argument no  yeah i think the design of universal  credit was absolutely right it is to get  people  to take personal responsibility for the  lives that they lead  um many of the people that are on on  universal credit particularly the people  that are  uh have been homeless have led chaotic  lives and therefore  need to understand what it's like to  receive a salary  and manage their money which is is  absolutely right  however we have a pandemic the like of  which we've never seen in our lifetimes  and i do think that there's a problem  here under normal circumstances  i would say no pay the money to to the  the tenant  they must then exercise uh their duty  and that is to pay their rent  and manage their finances we're in a  position whereby  a lot of people have reached the stage  of saying i can't manage my finances  um and should i pay my rent or should i  pay  should i buy food for the kids this is  the real problem here  that people are in a position whereby  they cannot manage  and so i would say look in this these  circumstances  and for a limited period of time then  that money should automatically go  to the landlord to at least safeguard  their  their housing because we pay i mean the  taxpayer is paying this money for the  rent  and therefore the rent must go to to the  landlord and if it doesn't  then uh we are potentially risking the  people that are  uh investing in property and letting it  out uh which will be a complete disaster  because many of those people  have chosen that route to supplement  their pensions  and therefore they're suffering from not  getting the money that they're due  so i think this is a i i understand  completely  the design i i it's one i support  strongly but i do  think that in these particular  circumstances we have to take stringent  action  okay where do you stand on this  evictions question so  so i think um bob's really clearly um  illustrated the difference between  private renting and social renting so in  terms of the  um evictions uh ban we welcomed that  and as a sector we made an absolute  commitment that  ahead of the evictions ban coming in in  about six months ago  but made an absolute commitment that  that no resident would be evicted  um due to the pandemic causing them to  be inventories  and we've worked incredibly closely our  members with their residents  in terms of hardship funds supporting  residents to access the welfare that  they're entitled to in helping  them navigate that system uh in tribute  to dwp  and they have had a tremendous amount of  pressure on them over the last six  months  they have um processed millions of  claims and they've worked really closely  with the sector  they've also done a couple of of things  um that have really helped support  residents so an additional 20 pounds  um which does go towards food uh has  been vitally welcome in terms of the  universal credit allowance during this  time  um but they've also made changes to the  payment system  so there's a new and improved payment  system for social landlords where you  can have a direct payment  to the landlord of the housing benefit  of the universal credit housing  benefit portion of of that and that's  really helped us sustain tendencies it's  helped support  uh it's being rolled out at the moment  but it's helping support those social  residents  manage their income and protect their  livelihoods and sustain their tenancies  it is very very different to the private  rented sector  um and i think it just kind of  illustrates how they are quite different  things so the private rented sector is  being used as a safety net for people  who'd be so much better off in  in social rent that they would get that  support and i absolutely hear what bob's  saying around those those private sector  landlords  many of which have have one home and  it's their income too  and now in a very tricky position so  there needs to be rental rights you know  rent  protection for those renters but that  also needs to be protection for those  landlords  but it's very very different in the  social housing sector just also on the  evictions  um we whilst we did support the ban  we've also supported it being  lifted um and that is because of the  points that bob raised as well the  evictions are a last resort but when  you're in  lockdown and somebody is doing  anti-social behavior  and it's affecting you in your home your  community it's it's horrendous  and there needs to be a tool to be able  to prevent that um and similarly with  with domestic violence there has got to  be  a mechanism for getting a perpetrator  out of her home um but that is  exceptional circumstances for us  and our sector is not using it for for  rental reasons  uh john uh we've got a question here  about the rent in london becoming  unaffordable for young people i mean  that comes back to the point you were  making  right at the beginning about regional  inequality and what the planning system  can do  to address that what you know the  government talks all about leveling up  how do you see that 