 worried about you  know the  way that basically destroys the  character of these neighborhoods  so for us the the most popular  thing we've come across is if you show  people  type types of housing that they've seen  in the past whether they be terraced  houses  or perhaps mansion blocks so think of  bath  or um or edinburgh or  all over the country you'll see  traditional types of housing and they  don't have to be a traditional design  you can have a perfectly  a contemporary street that's still  walkable and people are happy to live in  it  um those are the sorts of things that  people are very happy to accept and so  that's why we think if we're going to  add more homes  and if you don't want to touch more of  the countryside the easiest way to do  that is to find ways that local  residents can live with adding  more of the types of things they're used  to townhouses and mansion blocks  that don't stick up into the skyline  which is not to say by the way i mean i  was talking with the housing campaign  yesterday who was very happy in the her  flat in the new 30 story  30 story tower she just moved into so i  mean there are people who want to live  like that  but um local people can get upset so  that the easiest way to do therefore  is to just is to add more density  in ways that everyone is already used to  do you think  that this realistically um can this be  done without  building on bits of a green belt it  would be possible to completely end the  housing crisis  without building on the green belt  whether we will or not is an entirely  different question but you could add  you could easily add five million homes  in london alone  without touching a square inch of the  green belt now whether we can solve the  political difficulties whether we have  the will to do that is an entirely  different question  right but because it does strike me that  whenever i hear these plans if you're  not going to even build on the green  belt near commuter rail stations for 