 to pop out  you know walk to get a pint of milk  those kind of basics i think is what  beauty is in terms of place not just  statics but in terms of going down i  don't know uh if that's the right  solution for us  and what where where do you stand on the  question of  how many of you obviously represent  people in social housing  do you think that the the whole vision  of the kind of property owning democracy  the ultimate you know the classic tory  that's right mcmillanite view  but the ultimate aspiration is home  ownership do you do you think that is  now outdated or do you think that is  still you know what what most of tenants  in social housing would  if their financial circumstances improve  would like to do  so i think for um a significant  a vast proportion of the population  people would love to live in their own  home  and they'd love to run their home with a  bit of outdoor space um  and they'd like it to be in an area  where they had access to work into  schools  and um but i think the reality  is it's just really really really far  out of reach for millions of people  and so what we need to do is ensure that  everybody is well housed whether they  are social renting private rented or in  some form of the housing matter but we  have a  huge huge need for social housing as  part of that  um just because you're you're in social  housing doesn't mean that you can't move  out of it either so i think as a sector  yes  um we support the building of social  homes but we also are really positive  about home ownership as well it's  absolutely not  an either rule we need all of it but  it's in terms of the government  conversation it's where they direct  investment but also where they direct  investment right now in the midst of a  pandemic and a recovery  um because actually that building of  private homes at the moment is going to  be far harder in tougher economic  circumstances  and you're subject to mortgage  availability and everything else that's  going 